The exception message template `UNABLE_TO_WRITE_PROPERTY` expects the *expected* type of the property as its third argument, but the code currently passes `target` (which is the *actual* type of the provided `propertyValue`). This results in a misleading exception message like "expected type was 'java.lang.String'" when a `String` was provided but an `Integer` was expected.

We should pass the actual expected type of the property, which is `writeMethod.getParameterTypes()[0].getName()`. Additionally, `UNABLE_TO_WRITE_PROPERTY_RUNTIME` only has two placeholders, so we don't need to pass `target` to it.

```suggestion
        var expectedType = writeMethod.getParameterTypes()[0].getName();
        try {
            var result = writeMethod.invoke(bean, propertyValue);
            return Objects.requireNonNullElse(result, bean);
        } catch (IllegalAccessException e) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    MoreStrings.lenientFormat(UNABLE_TO_WRITE_PROPERTY, propertyName, bean.getClass(), expectedType), e);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    MoreStrings.lenientFormat(UNABLE_TO_WRITE_PROPERTY, propertyName, bean.getClass(), expectedType), e);
        } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                    MoreStrings.lenientFormat(UNABLE_TO_WRITE_PROPERTY_RUNTIME, propertyName, bean.getClass()),
                    e);
        }
```
